 functions as a request.","keywords":["polite-modal-request"],"skill":"polite-modal-request","contextKey":"imperatives-49","fingerprint":"6814e57e3e750fdc7bf7b0538e16624ff10458394fca652799e9cc5799018deb","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verbs-reference"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Could makes the request less direct, but the following verb still uses its **base form**.","After modal **could**, use base **prepare**. The question functions as a request.","Choose **prepare**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Could requires the base verb here, not an -ing form.","B":"There is no to after could in this request.","C":"After modal **could**, use base **prepare**. The question functions as a request.","D":"A modal is not followed by a third-person -s form."}}},{"id":"ac94f27e-8e3a-561f-ab74-14ef4833feb3","topicSlug":"imperatives","level":"A1","prompt":"Give a direct instruction: “____ the rowing boat, please.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"Repaired"},{"id":"B","text":"To repair"},{"id":"C","text":"Repair"},{"id":"D","text":"Repairs"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Use a **base verb** for the instruction.","explanation":"An imperative starts with the base verb **repair**; the subject you is understood.","keywords":["positive-command"],"skill":"positive-command","contextKey":"imperatives-50","fingerprint":"047320d2da45e6e06dd69a388742c77d3255d88ffe03591485f4f27a604f5514","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verbs-reference"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Use a **base verb** for the instruction.","An imperative starts with the base verb **repair**; the subject you is understood.","Choose **Repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A past form does not make a present instruction.","B":"A to-infinitive is not the ordinary direct imperative.","C":"An imperative starts with the base verb **repair**; the subject you is understood.","D":"An imperative does not take third-person -s."}}},{"id":"dbf0ce5c-76b1-5aed-a3fd-1167d4a276ef","topicSlug":"imperatives","level":"A1","prompt":"Give a negative instruction: “Do not ____ the rowing boat yet.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repairs"},{"id":"B","text":"repairing"},{"id":"C","text":"to repair"},{"id":"D","text":"repair"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Build **do not + base verb**.","explanation":"After **do not**, keep the base form **repair**.","keywords":["negative-command"],"skill":"negative-command","contextKey":"imperatives-50","fingerprint":"ceac3a1dfaa8a09eb5e7ddbe9999dd223b277664a1ee0411f4bdfeccbe0dfa4c","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verbs-reference"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Build **do not + base verb**.","After **do not**, keep the base form **repair**.","Choose **repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Do not already supplies the auxiliary; remove third-person -s.","B":"The imperative after do not needs the base verb, not -ing.","C":"Do not takes a bare infinitive, without to.","D":"After **do not**, keep the base form **repair**."}}},{"id":"55028225-c983-50fc-abe5-8b4e4790ee44","topicSlug":"imperatives","level":"A1","prompt":"Suggest doing the task together: “Let’s ____ the rowing boat now.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"repairs"},{"id":"C","text":"to repair"},{"id":"D","text":"repair"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"After **let’s**, choose the bare infinitive.","explanation":"**Let’s** takes base **repair** and includes the speaker in the suggestion.","keywords":["shared-suggestion"],"skill":"shared-suggestion","contextKey":"imperatives-50","fingerprint":"48bae0c281ccadeef3cb7a5c5cb437563e264b60fac402de2d9516d97a8e22bd","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verbs-reference"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["After **let’s**, choose the bare infinitive.","**Let’s** takes base **repair** and includes the speaker in the suggestion.","Choose **repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The -ing form does not follow let’s directly.","B":"Third-person agreement is not used after let’s.","C":"Let’s takes an infinitive without to.","D":"**Let’s** takes base **repair** and includes the speaker in the suggestion."}}},{"id":"641b8a73-511a-53bb-ac6c-4c2c7b85fd29","topicSlug":"imperatives","level":"A1","prompt":"Complete the polite request: “Could you ____ the rowing boat, please?”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to repair"},{"id":"B","text":"repair"},{"id":"C","text":"repairs"},{"id":"D","text":"repairing"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Could makes the request less direct, but the following verb still uses its **base form**.","explanation":"After modal **could**, use base **repair**. The question functions as a request.","keywords":["polite-modal-request"],"skill":"polite-modal-request","contextKey":"imperatives-50","fingerprint":"d4e2ccd5fea6a564a28458ca5228e44955df9380711ecb7c6fb848822be7a744","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-verbs-reference"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Could makes the request less direct, but the following verb still uses its **base form**.","After modal **could**, use base **repair**. The question functions as a request.","Choose **repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"There is no to after could in this request.","B":"After modal **could**, use base **repair**. The question functions as a request.","C":"A modal is not followed by a third-person -s form.","D":"Could requires the base verb here, not an -ing form."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Give instructions with a base verb, make them negative, and suggest actions together.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"The unspoken subject","explanation":"An imperative often begins with the **base verb**. Its subject you is usually understood. This lets instructions be short without being incomplete sentences. Tone and context determine whether an imperative is helpful, firm or rude.","pattern":"**Open** the window. / **Please wait** here.","commonMistakes":["Do not add -s to an imperative, even when one person receives the instruction."],"examples":["Give a direct instruction: “**Prepare** the visitor guide, please.”","Give a direct instruction: “**Restore** the old portrait, please.”"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Negative instructions","explanation":"Use **do not / don’t + base verb** for a prohibition or negative instruction. This also applies to be in a command: Don’t be late. Ordinary statements with be have a different negative pattern.","pattern":"**Don’t touch** the switch. / **Do not be** late.","commonMistakes":["Not touch the switch is not the usual full imperative form."],"examples":["Give a negative instruction: “Do not **prepare** the visitor guide yet.”","Give a negative instruction: “Do not **restore** the old portrait yet.”"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Suggestions with let’s","explanation":"Use **let’s + base verb** to propose an action involving the speaker and other people. Let’s is a contraction of let us in this use. The negative is usually let’s not + base verb.","pattern":"**Let’s take** a break. / **Let’s not hurry**.","commonMistakes":["Lets without an apostrophe is a different verb form: she lets us leave."],"examples":["Suggest doing the task together: “Let’s **prepare** the visitor guide now.”","Suggest doing the task together: “Let’s **restore** the old portrait now.”"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Polite requests","explanation":"Please can soften an instruction. Modal questions with could or would often make a request less direct. After could you or would you, retain the base verb. Please does not make every command appropriate; relationship and situation matter.","pattern":"**Could you close** the door, please?","commonMistakes":["Would you mind takes an -ing form: Would you mind closing the door? It is a different pattern."],"examples":["Complete the polite request: “Could you **prepare** the visitor guide, please?”","Complete the polite request: “Could you **restore** the old portrait, please?”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["**Open** the window. / **Please wait** here.","**Don’t touch** the switch. / **Do not be** late.","**Let’s take** a break. / **Let’s not hurry**.","**Could you close** the door, please?"]},"stage":"1 · Sentence foundations","prerequisites":["question-forms"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
